# Review ownership for this repository, referenced by ADR-003, ADR-005 and
# ADR-006: content edits to a contract need only its own area's owners; schema
# and tooling changes route to the platform team.
#
# GitHub reads CODEOWNERS from the repo root (or .github/, both are valid;
# root is used here to match this repo's flat top level). Last matching
# pattern wins, so more specific paths are listed after broader ones.
#
# Usernames below are placeholders matching owner_registry.py's fake
# business-application registry (Owner.solution_owner), not real GitHub
# accounts -- see owner_registry.py's own docstring for what a production
# mapping would resolve against instead (an Entra ID / identity-provider
# group per business-application id, not a hand-maintained file like this
# one). Because of that, `hecinmontion/ucmetadata-demo`'s real branch
# protection on `main` cannot require review *from these specific owners* --
# none of them are real GitHub accounts capable of approving anything, and
# the repository's only real collaborator is its own author, so requiring
# review at all (from anyone) would deadlock every future merge. What's
# actually turned on instead (2026-09-20): a pull request is required before
# merging, and the `validate` check must pass -- enforced even for the
# repository admin, no bypass. This file still does its real job of
# *routing* which path a change takes (ADR-006); it just isn't the thing a
# GitHub setting can point at to require a specific human's approval, in
# this particular solo repository. What this file does NOT express: the
# second, distinct approver Rules & Constraints requires for sensitivity/PII
# changes -- CODEOWNERS alone can name required reviewers per path, but a
# genuine "two different humans must approve" gate needs a real second
# reviewer to exist, which this repository does not have.

# Default: unmatched paths fall to the platform team.
*                                       @data-platform-team

# Content edits -- fast path (ADR-006): each dataset's own owning team.
/contracts/analytics/customers.yaml    @priya-natarajan
/contracts/analytics/orders.yaml       @grace-okafor
/contracts/marketing/campaigns.yaml    @marco-silva
/contracts/marketing/leads.yaml        @marco-silva

# Schema and tooling -- slow path (ADR-006): the platform team owns the
# contract shape, the CLI, CI, and everything the design depends on, per
# "platform owns zero content" (spec Users & Roles).
